\subsection{Similarity-weighted aggregation}
Weighting neighbors by embedding similarity removed most of that loss (Table~\ref{tab:ap}). The untuned $3\times3$ rule reached $F=$ @F_SIMW@, @D_SIMW_MEAN@ above the fixed mean and @D_SIMW_NONE@ above no smoothing (paired 95\% intervals). It kept the region recall of scattered anomalies at @R_SIMW_SC@ and of bars at @R_SIMW_BAR@, lowered the false regions per image from @FR_NONE@ (no smoothing, averaged over the controlled shapes) to @FR_SIMW@, and raised the DINOv2 image AP to @T_SIMW_D@. It did not help the handcrafted-feature images (@T_SIMW_H@, against @T_NONE_H@ without smoothing), and on the rotor-only lattices it stayed slightly below no smoothing (@T_SIMW_ROT@ against @T_NONE_ROT@). The one-sided rule matched no smoothing (@D_ONE_NONE@ in $F$).

\subsection{Order gates and the von Mises--Fisher information}
The development-selected order gates did not beat ordinary similarity: the gate on compatibility reached $F=$ @F_OGCOS@, the gates on the resultant length and on $H_\chi$ @F_OGVMF@, against @F_SIMW@ for the untuned similarity-weighted rule (@D_OGCOS_SIMW@ and @D_OGVMF_SIMW@, paired intervals). The last two gave the same scores to within 0.01 in every scenario, as expected from the monotone relation of $H_\chi$ and $R_i$ (the small differences come from the numerical inversion of $A_d$). The $d$-dimensional entropy extension is therefore reported as a formal result; it did not improve detection in these experiments.

