\subsection{Two further ideas}
The nucleation-and-growth rule, with its parameters selected on the development scenarios, reached $F=$ @F_NGH@ on the test scenarios: the same as no smoothing (paired difference @D_NGH_NONE@) and below the similarity-weighted rule (@D_NGH_SIMW@). A label-free \emph{closure gap}, motivated by the failure of mean-field closures that average a steep function over a heterogeneous neighborhood, is the share of the cohort's top 3\% sites that are still in the top 3\% after the fixed mean (the retention). It correlated with the effect of the fixed mean on the average precision over the @N_COH@ development and test cohorts (Spearman $\rho=$ @CG_RHO@, $p=$ @CG_P@), but a choice between the fixed mean and no smoothing based on it, fitted on the development cohorts, selected the mean for @CG_SHARE@ of the test cohorts and reached a mean AP of @CG_CHOICE@, against @CG_NONE@ for no smoothing (oracle choice @CG_ORACLE@) and @CG_SIMW@ for similarity weighting, which makes the choice unnecessary. As a robustness criterion in the spirit of grid convergence, the range of the average precision over the six anomaly shapes was @RG_NONE@ without smoothing, @RG_MEAN@ for the fixed mean and @RG_SIMW@ for the similarity-weighted rule.

