\section{Limitations and future work}
The lattices and the images are simulated and were written by the author of the method (the simulation is MyUncle's and its parameter sets are not fitted to measurements; the controlled generator has diverse, separable anomalies and Gaussian noise); nothing here shows that the method finds anything in an experimental system. Development and test data come from the same generator families, @TEST_UNITS@ independent units per test scenario are few, the rule parameters are selected with labels, and the handcrafted-feature images are not helped by any rule. Novelty is not a defect: a rare state may be a rare preparation or a known feature that is merely uncommon. The comparison of the rarity score itself with other detectors (isolation forest, local outlier factor, nearest-neighbor and Mahalanobis distance) was made under the earlier fixed smoothing and is in the repository: a smoothed Mahalanobis baseline scored higher than the rarity score on the development images, so the score is not claimed to be the best detector, and the contribution concerns the aggregation step. Planned: validation on a public data set and on experimental micrographs with independently established defects, blinded expert review, more units per scenario, other filters of the bilateral and non-local-means families as baselines, and a label-free choice between smoothing and no smoothing (the autocorrelation of the unsmoothed score is a candidate; in a first look it separated the two lattice scenarios in which the fixed mean destroyed the signal from the rest, with eight scenarios and one threshold, which is a hypothesis, not a rule).

\section{Conclusions}
A fixed smoothing window can destroy an anomaly signal, depending on the size, shape and scatter of the anomalies and on the embedding. Aggregation weighted by embedding similarity, with a label-free scale, was a robust replacement on independent test scenarios (paired intervals exclude zero). Gates based on local order and on the von Mises--Fisher information, the $d$-dimensional extension of MyUncle's entropy quantities, did not improve on ordinary similarity, and an evolutionary search over rule configurations did not beat random search. All evidence is simulated; experimental validation is the next step.

